      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Chief Justice Charles Evans Hughes, urban planner Robert Moses, and Olympic swimmer Gertrude Ederle are among the famous figures buried at Woodlawn.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodlawn_Cemetery_(Bronx%2C_New_York)",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Barnabas Church, founded in 1910, shielded the cemetery from widespread arson and abandonment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/woodlawn-heights",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Woodlawn Heights Taxpayers and Community Association, founded in the 1920s, preserved the neighborhood’s low‑rise, residential character amid Bronx urbanization.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/woodlawn-heights",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The New York and Harlem Railroad, built along the Bronx River, sparked early residential development on Webster and Katonah Avenues.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
